In a couple of our projects, we are using the `retry` method of the Http Client to handle authentication errors. If a 401 error is thrown, we request a new token and then retry.
To achieve this, we currently have to manually create a request variable and pass the variable inside the closure in order to modify the request and set a new token on the request.
```php
($request = Http::baseUrl(...))
  ->withToken($this->getToken())
  ->retry(2, 0, function ($exception) use ($request) {
      if ($exception->response->status() !== 401) {
          return false;
      }
  
      $request->withToken($this->getNewToken());
  
      return true;
  })
  ->get('...');
```
This PR allows you to access the request as a parameter of the closure:
```php
Http::baseUrl(...)
  ->withToken($this->getToken())
  ->retry(2, 0, function ($exception, $request) { 
      if ($exception->response->status() !== 401) {
          return false;
      }
  
      $request->withToken($this->getNewToken());
  
      return true;
  })
  ->get('...');
```

This PR integrates the contract of the bridge and makes it accessible through a new `bridge` React Context.

To test it you will need to start a local HardHat network and deploy the contracts of the bridge. [Follow these instructions](https://github.com/hermeznetwork/internal-tools/tree/main/FE-tools).

You will also need to set up this network in MetaMask:

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/15896177/161325704-ec9d9b49-255e-417b-8163-fac52e87f5e3.png)

And add this code to the Home page:

```typescript
import { useEffect } from "react";
import { useBridgeContext } from "src/contexts/bridge.context";
import { BigNumber } from "ethers";
import { ethers } from "ethers";

const { bridge, claim } = useBridgeContext();

useEffect(() => {
  const amount = BigNumber.from("0x0de0b6b3a7640000");
  const bridgeDestinationNetwork = 1;
  const destinationAddress = "0x730F01feBde4caBcBa8240020854610BAC89BAb2";
  const claimOriginalNetwork = "1";
  const claimDestinationNetwork = 0;
  const smtpProof = (() => {
    try {
      return JSON.parse(prompt("smtpProof") || "[]") as Array<string>;
    } catch (error) {
      return [];
    }
  })(); // from API's merkle_proof
  const globalExitRootNum = parseInt(prompt("globalExitRootNum") || "0"); // from API's exit_root_num
  if (isNaN(globalExitRootNum) === false) {
    const mainnetExitRoot = prompt("mainnetExitRoot") || ""; // from API's main_exit_root
    const rollupExitRoot = prompt("rollupExitRoot") || ""; // from API's rollup_exit_root
    void claim(
      ethers.constants.AddressZero,
      amount,
      claimOriginalNetwork,
      claimDestinationNetwork,
      destinationAddress,
      smtpProof,
      globalExitRootNum,
      mainnetExitRoot,
      rollupExitRoot
    )
      .then((claimTx) => {
        console.log("Claim response TX:", claimTx);
        void bridge(
          ethers.constants.AddressZero,
          amount,
          bridgeDestinationNetwork,
          destinationAddress
        )
          .then((bridgeTx) => {
            console.log("Bridge response TX:", bridgeTx);
          })
          .catch(console.error);
      })
      .catch(console.error);
  }
}, [bridge, claim]);
```

After logging in, the UI will prompt for a `smtpProof`, a `globalExitRootNum`, a `mainnetExitRoot` and a `rollupExitRoot`. You can get this data from the file `claim_output.json` that is generated by the command `node generateClaimBridge.js -o 1 -a 1 -d 0x730F01feBde4caBcBa8240020854610BAC89BAb2` of the FE-tools. You can `cat claim_output.json` and copy each value.

When these four parameters are provided, MetaMask will ask you to sign both transactions. The result will be logged in the console.

https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/15896177/161330916-f4e93f47-6b32-43ba-99a2-49f428b23e74.mp4

In case you're getting a "Nonce too high" error, reset your MM account as follows:

